{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content -Raw docs/reviews/wi-960/005-ADJUDICATE-2373372.md'","aggregated_output":"# WI-960 checkpoint sitting at 2373372\n\n## amendment\n\nAll three rows changed MEANING against their anchor (commit 802f72327): each adds obligations the blessed text did not carry. I read each against its chain, the tests it cites, and the code at this tip. Two I would bless. One I would not, because its new sentence contradicts a clause it keeps. The rung is released, so the later act turn re-attests only the two marked MEANING+BLESS.\n\n- [MEANING+BLESS] LLR-269 Detail -> before: one session service launches and records every model call, the record operation writing one session log and its own telemetry commit wherever it runs -> after: the same, plus:\n  - the record operation commits a log only from a claimed linked worktree, including one whose local claim closed while the shared tree still carries its active work;\n  - from the shared checkout, a detached checkout or an unclaimed branch, it writes the log to the primary checkout's spool and commits nothing;\n  - an unreadable claim read adds no claim, and spools only when neither readable tree establishes the claim;\n  - a spool write failure raises without a commit, and a refused telemetry commit is reported to the caller;\n  - retained-session input beside a non-empty resume identifier is refused before accounting, while an unretained resume records the resumed mode without retaining the identifier;\n  - call-log names stay distinct from gate-log names.\n\n  -> not the same: a correct implementation of the old text commits from any root, which the new text forbids. Ready to bless. Each clause is an observable input, outcome or refusal, fail-closed where the claim cannot be read (it spools rather than commits). It names no new code symbol. TC-265 and TC-384 drive each arm, and the code agrees (a primary-rooted call spools and leaves HEAD unmoved).\n- [MEANING+BLESS] TC-265 Method -> before: the service's act and call steps, the session log and commit, and the two structural guards -> after: the same, plus: record driven from claimed and out-of-lane roots (a claimed worktree, the close-first case included, commits; the shared checkout, a detached checkout, an unclaimed branch and an unreadable lane reading spool without a commit); an unwritable spool raises; a refused commit is reported; a resumed call's form and recorded mode; retained-session input beside a resume identifier refused before accounting; call-log names distinct from gate-log names; the three retired shell launchers referred to nowhere -> not the same: new arms enter the verified set. Ready to bless. Each arm matches a new LLR-269 clause one for one, and every evidence node this sitting checked exists.\n- [MEANING] LLR-270 Detail -> before: the keep operation, its store, leases, markers, blackout, keep-warm and review retention, the keep-warm ping's session log \"written and committed by the dispatcher's own thread between polls and only over a clean trunk, else held and said\" -> after: the same, plus \"A dispatcher-rooted ping records through the service and spools its log because the dispatcher's primary checkout is not a lane\" -> not the same: a ping that used to commit now commits nothing. NOT blessed, for one reason. The row now says both things about one log. The kept sentence still says the ping's log is committed by the dispatcher's thread over a clean trunk, else held. The new sentence says that same ping spools and commits nothing. The dispatcher's warmer is always rooted at the primary checkout (the new test, `test_a_keep_warm_ping_from_the_primary_checkout_spools_its_log`, asserts no commit and an unmoved HEAD), so the kept commit clause can no longer describe any ping, and whether the clean-trunk hold still governs a spool write is left unsaid. A reader cannot tell which sentence binds. The same stale claim stands in approved TC-268's Method (\"the next tick writes its session log and commits it on the tick's own thread\"), which this lane did not amend. Rewrite the keep-warm clause so it states the one behaviour, spooled with or without the hold, and amend TC-268 with it.\n\nVERDICT: MEANING rows=3\n\n## first-approval\n\nFour lane-added rows await a first approval: SR-243, LLR-346, TC-384 and TC-385. I read them against SN-026, the sibling SRs (SR-222, SR-227, SR-239), LLR-269 and LLR-270, the lane's arms map, and the tests. All 48 evidence nodes exist. Tracing this repository's own coordinator tooling is consistent with the approved spine (LLR-340 and LLR-301 already do it), so that is not a finding.\n\nThe chain is not ready. It has one missing record and three text findings, and the rows go back together.\n\n- [RETURN] SR-243 -> the obligation: when the coordinator's tooling launches a model session, record it in the usage record and, when it starts outside a claimed unit of work, record its log without a shared commit. Its acceptance adds:\n  - claimed work defined to include the close-first case;\n  - a refused record commit reported, never success;\n  - a resumed authoring turn continuing its recorded thread and refusing a missing, damaged, foreign or newer-uncommitted carrier;\n  - an edit check that tells an edit from another call's record commit and treats unreadable history as unable to check.\n\n  -> what its chain shows: the row is honestly labelled DERIVED. Its two lenses fit: UNATTENDED-OPS (unrecoverable unattended progress) and INTEGRITY-RECOVERABILITY (telemetry committed to unrelated shared work). Its Rationale argues the lenses and the rejected alternatives and feeds back to SN-026. The `When` is fronted, and no cell names a file -> why it is not ready, two findings:\n  - (1) **No perspective record.** No `*.perspectives.toml` covers this lane's rows. WI-957's and WI-958's lanes each answered this return with `hats.py record`, and the same applies here.\n  - (2) **The acceptance carries obligations the Requirement does not state.** The Requirement obliges recording and the no-commit rule. The resume-carrier refusal and the whole edit check (distinguishing an edit from another call's record commit, and treating unreadable history as unable to check) appear only in the acceptance. An acceptance cell holds the fit criterion of its requirement; it cannot mint one (SR-026's Rationale records this repository's own ruling on exactly this). Either widen the Requirement to state the resume and edit-judgement obligations, or move them to their own row.\n- [RETURN] LLR-346 -> the obligation:\n  - resolve a declared coordinator role to an enabled route and launch under its sandbox, model, effort and prompt input, refusing unknown roles, unreadable prompts, unavailable routes, out-of-lane authoring items and invalid resume carriers before launch;\n  - snapshot head and uncommitted state before and after, and judge edits under the stated discount rules;\n  - select a resume carrier by the newest standard call log;\n  - record every outcome, and return distinct results for refusal, edit, refused commit and unable check;\n  - have published coordinator guidance name this route as the only Sol or Terra launch path.\n\n  -> what its chain shows: the edit-judgement and resume rules are precise, closed and fail-closed (a non-descendant head is an edit, unreadable history is unable to check), and TC-384 drives them -> why it is not ready, two findings:\n  - (1) **A `shall` in an LLR.** The row opens \"A coordinator call shall resolve its declared role ...\". The SR states the obligation and the LLR decomposes it, so the design row states its contract without `shall`.\n  - (2) **A second decision its parent does not ask for.** The closing clause, published guidance naming this call as the only Sol or Terra launch path and describing its blackout refusal, results and spooling, is a documentation obligation on a different artifact, verified by its own TC (TC-385). SR-243 says nothing about guidance. Either argue that clause under SR-243 or give it its own home, so this row states one decision. This is the finding WI-957's sitting 003 returned for the same shape.\n- [RETURN] TC-384 -> the obligation: drive each coordinator role against enabled, disabled and unknown rows; every before-and-after edit state for review, critique and authoring calls; first and resumed authoring turns over every bad carrier; an out-of-lane authoring item; an interrupted launch; and every lane, shared, detached, unclaimed, close-first, spool-write and refused-commit state -> what its chain shows: a thorough Method over 47 named, existing cases verifying SR-243, LLR-346, LLR-269 and IF-246 -> why it is not ready: its Expected states the recording, spooling and resume-selection conditions, but not the edit judgement. That judgement is the bulk of LLR-346, and SR-243's acceptance names it: an edit told apart from another call's record commit, a non-descendant head as an edit, unreadable history as unable to check, and none of these ever success. Nor does Expected state that a refused record commit is never success. The cases hold those arms, but the cell does not claim them. Add both to Expected, adjusted to whatever SR-243's rework keeps.\n- [RETURN] TC-385 -> the obligation: read the coordinator-cycle guidance and its synchronized copies for the required call invocation, the blackout refusal, outcome meanings, spool behaviour, and the retirement of direct Sol and Terra launch recipes -> what its chain shows: its two evidence nodes exist, and Smoke is honest for a text pin -> why it is not ready: its own text is sound, but it verifies exactly LLR-346's guidance clause, which finding (2) above asks to justify or re-home. Approving it now would anchor a `Verifies` cell the rework may move. It re-sits with the set.\n\nOUTCOME: RETURN rows=4\n\n## Dispositions\n\n```toml\ntitle = \"WI-960 spine rework: SR-243's perspective record and requirement scope, LLR-346's shall and guidance clause, TC-384's Expected, LLR-270's keep-warm log clause with TC-268\"\nworkstream = \"process\"\nsr_refs = [\"SR-243\", \"SR-227\"]\nneeds = [\"WI-960\"]\nsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\nbuildtier = \"medium\"\n```\n\nThis is spine text, plus one record file. No code change is called for.\n1. **The perspective record:** write the lane's perspective record (`python project-trajectory/scripts/hats.py record docs/plans/WI-960/plan.perspectives.toml --row SR-243 --row LLR-346 --by <who>`), with a no-finding per applicable hat. It must pass `--check --strict`.\n2. **SR-243:** state the resume-carrier refusal and the edit-judgement obligations in its Requirement, or move them to their own row, so its acceptance only measures what the Requirement obliges.\n3. **LLR-346:** state its contract without `shall`, and argue its published-guidance clause under SR-243 or re-home it, re-pointing TC-385's `Verifies` to match.\n4. **TC-384:** add the edit-judgement outcomes and the refused-commit-never-success outcome to Expected.\n5. **LLR-270:** rewrite the keep-warm logging clause so it states one behaviour for the dispatcher-rooted ping (spooled, with or without the clean-trunk hold), and amend TC-268's Method to match.\n\nThen re-sit LLR-270 and TC-268 for the amendment, and SR-243, LLR-346, TC-384 and TC-385 for first approval. LLR-269's and TC-265's blessings stand, because none of these changes touches their text.\n\nSITTING: JUDGED kinds=amendment;first-approval\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
